  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "When dealing with linear models for complex designs, it is not immediately clear if the resulting OLS/GLS equations have solutions. Generalized inverses provide a check of consistency, and hence of whether a system of equations has any solutions. A linear system $ \\mathbf{A}\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y} $ is consistent if and only if",
      "source_page": 848,
      "printed_page": 828,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p848:b16"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        141.0,
        1145.0,
        1101.0,
        1277.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula103]]",
      "source_page": 848,
      "printed_page": 828,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p848:b17"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        551.0,
        1298.0,
        695.0,
        1332.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Given a consistent system, all solutions have the form",
      "source_page": 848,
      "printed_page": 828,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p848:b19"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        143.0,
