    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "where c is an arbitrary $ q \\times 1 $ column vector. For example, taking c = 0 recovers Equation A3.2, while if $ A^{-1} $ exists, then $ I - A^{-1}A = 0 $ and the solution $ x = A^{-1}y $ is unique. To see that any expression of the form of Equation A3.4 is a solution, note that",
      "source_page": 848,
      "printed_page": 828,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p848:b22",
        "p849:b2"
      ],
      "bbox": [
