    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Although an infinite number of solutions exists when A is singular, particular linear combinations (or contrasts) of the elements of x may have unique values. For example, consider the system $ x_{1} + x_{2} = 1 $. Here there are an infinite number of solutions for $ (x_{1}, x_{2}) $, but only a single solution, 1, for the contrast $ x_{1} + x_{2} $.",
      "source_page": 850,
      "printed_page": 830,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p850:b5"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        142.0,
        448.0,
        1099.0,
        577.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "Consider some linear combination $ \\mathbf{b}^{T}\\mathbf{x} $. If the vector of constants $ \\mathbf{b} $ satisfies",
      "source_page": 850,
      "printed_page": 830,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p850:b6"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        193.0,
        578.0,
        1096.0,
        611.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "[[FORMULA:Genetics_appendix1_formula114]]",
      "source_page": 850,
      "printed_page": 830,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p850:b7"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        530.0,
        637.0,
        708.0,
        673.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "then $ b^{T}x $ has a unique solution given by",
      "source_page": 850,
      "printed_page": 830,
      "source_block_ids": [
        "p850:b9"
      ],
      "bbox": [
        141.0,
        704.0,
